8153173,2024-01-12,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"As stated in the Fair Credit Reporting Act 15 USC 1681 Section 602, it is decisive for consumer reporting agencies like XXXX and Experian to carry out their responsibilities with fairness, unbiased, and respect for the consumer 's right to privacy. As the consumer, I have the right to make certain that my private information is not shared, as supported by section 6801 of the same Act. This section emphasizes that financial institutions, including the entity furnishing information to credit agencies, have an ongoing obligation to protect the privacy and confidentiality of their customers ' nonpublic personal information. Section 604 ( a ) ( 2 ) of the Act states that consumer reporting agencies may only furnish a consumer report under specific circumstances, one of which is when they receive written instructions from the consumer to whom the report relates. In this case, neither the financial institution nor the consumer reporting agencies, XXXX and Experian, have my consent to furnish this information, let alone my written consent. Therefore, any previous consent given to XXXX, Experian, or the furnisher of information to credit agencies, whether it was verbal, non-verbal, written, implied, or otherwise, is hereby revoked. Furthermore, section 6802 ( b ) ( c ) of the Act states that a financial institution can not impart nonpublic personal information to a nonaffiliated third party unless the consumer is provided with an explanation of how they can exercise their right to nondisclosure. However, the furnisher of information to credit agencies failed to inform me of my right to exercise this nondisclosure option. Furthermore, section 1681C ( a ) ( 5 ) specifies that no consumer reporting agency may include any adverse item of information in a consumer report, except as authorized under subsection ( b ). In 