8223839,2024-01-25,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Account status incorrect,"I trust this letter finds you well. I am writing in response to my recent Credit Reports sent by XXXX, XXXX XXXX TRANSUNION which brought to my attention certain discrepancies regarding my accounts with XXXX XXXX. Upon reviewing my credit report, I observed that information has been furnished, specifically pertaining to the credit card accounts.

I would like to draw your attention to 15 USC 1692a ( 2 ), where "" Communication '' is defined as the conveying of information regarding a debt, directly or indirectly, through any medium. In accordance with this definition, any medium includes oral, written, electronic, or other means, encompassing nonaffiliated third parties, such as the three major CRAs : XXXX, XXXX, and TRANSUNION. Consequently, the act of furnishing the Credit Reports by XXXX XXXX qualifies as a form of Communication. 

In light of the aforementioned legal provisions, I hereby demand that you Cease and Desist from disclosing my nonpublic personal information to all nonaffiliated third parties, including XXXX, XXXX, and TRANSUNION. I would like to emphasize that I have not provided consent for your company to share my nonpublic personal information, and I request clarification on any nondisclosure clause within your contract/agreement that grants such authorization. 

Referring to 15 USC 6801 ( a ), it is emphasized that financial institutions bear an affirmative and continuing obligation to respect the privacy of their customers and safeguard the security and confidentiality of nonpublic personal information. As per 15 USC 6802 ( B ), a financial institution is prohibited from disclosing nonpublic personal information to nonaffiliated third parties, unless the consumer is given the opportunity to direct against such disclosure and provided an explanation of the nondisclosure option. 

Moreover, I bring to your attention the Privacy Act of 1974 ( 5 U.S.C. 552a ) and the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( 15 U.S.C. 1681 et seq. ), which underscore the protection of personal information held by the federal government and consumer reporting agencies, respectively.

Furthermore, under 15 U.S. Code 1681s-2, furnishers of information to consumer reporting agencies are duty-bound to provide accurate information. It is explicitly prohibited to furnish information known to be inaccurate, and upon being notified of errors by the consumer, the inaccurate information must be rectified promptly. 

It has come to my notice that the information being reported is inaccurate, and I formally request that you rectify and delete all inaccurate information from my consumer report immediately. Additionally, I emphasize that the reporting of cancelled debt as a charge-off is in contradiction to IRS guidelines XXXX which define such events as gross or ordinary income, not to be reported on credit reports. 

